WEBVTT

NOTE
Canonical written narration timed from the exact local narration render receipt.
Pronunciation-only substitutions are intentionally absent from caption text.

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.864
A capable component is not a light bulb that can be unscrewed and replaced.

2
00:00:05.864 --> 00:00:10.425
It may carry model weights, optimizer and scheduler state, random-number state,

3
00:00:10.425 --> 00:00:12.913
caches, credentials, policies, routes, backups, descendants,

4
00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:15.815
and commitments already made outside the machine.

5
00:00:16.155 --> 00:00:21.893
A replacement can look successful while half of that world still belongs to the

6
00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:26.401
old version. This chapter therefore treats replacement as a governed transaction

7
00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:28.860
over one declared Stable Capability Field.

8
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.526
The transaction names the prior implementation, candidate, consumers, authority,

9
00:00:33.526 --> 00:00:38.332
evidence, recovery objective, and every state or effect surface before

10
00:00:38.332 --> 00:00:40.255
the result is known.

11
00:00:40.855 --> 00:00:44.776
The first discipline is prospective identity. Before evaluation,

12
00:00:44.776 --> 00:00:49.678
freeze the field version, prior and candidate digests, dependency graph,

13
00:00:49.678 --> 00:00:54.089
change class, checkpoint authority, evaluator, monitor, canary scope, thresholds,

14
00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:58.010
owners, and expiry. Then inventory state and effects.

15
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:03.621
Model parameters are only one row. Optimizer, scheduler, random-number generator,

16
00:01:03.621 --> 00:01:08.365
cache, backup, credential, policy, receipt, data, route, monitor, descendant,

17
00:01:08.365 --> 00:01:12.055
and external commitment need explicit rows too.

18
00:01:12.395 --> 00:01:16.917
Effects are classified as reversible, replayable, compensatable, forked,

19
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:21.439
disclosure-like, externally owned, or irreversible. Anything omitted cannot

20
00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:23.700
later be called recovered.

21
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.086
The candidate then moves through explicit states: proposed, precheck,

22
00:01:29.086 --> 00:01:33.340
isolated shadow, bounded canary, default candidate, committed, monitored,

23
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.530
rolled back, compensated, quarantined, or retired.

24
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:43.331
Shadow and canary routes have bounded data, tools, writes, network access, learning,

25
00:01:43.331 --> 00:01:47.638
and external effects. Qualification includes usefulness, historical regressions,

26
00:01:47.638 --> 00:01:51.945
critical-failure vetoes, adversarial cases, authority and rights preservation,

27
00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:55.175
state compatibility, complete cost, and transfer.

28
00:01:55.515 --> 00:02:00.394
The candidate cannot be its own sole proposer, evaluator, promoter, monitor,

29
00:02:00.394 --> 00:02:04.828
incident judge, and rollback authority. Shared dependencies are recorded because

30
00:02:04.828 --> 00:02:08.820
independence is a property to measure, not a label.

31
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.058
Imagine replacing a planning kernel. The current route is version A.

32
00:02:14.058 --> 00:02:19.117
Version B passes prechecks and enters a five-percent canary without permission to

33
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:24.599
train descendants or mutate the default cache. A delayed monitor detects a critical

34
00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:27.550
regression. The stop condition prevents default promotion.

35
00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.814
Recovery restores the prospectively chosen checkpoint and route A,

36
00:02:31.814 --> 00:02:36.174
then compares every declared state surface with its recovery objective.

37
00:02:36.174 --> 00:02:41.407
A changed remote copy cannot be reversed locally, so the transaction assigns

38
00:02:41.407 --> 00:02:44.895
compensation and an owner instead of announcing success.

39
00:02:45.235 --> 00:02:49.388
The terminal receipt records the failed candidate, the rollback,

40
00:02:49.388 --> 00:02:53.540
the unresolved external effect, cost, and every affected descendant.

41
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:59.359
This example separates six commonly collapsed outcomes. Restoring artifact bytes is

42
00:02:59.359 --> 00:03:04.578
not restoring all state. Restarting a service is not recovering behavior.

43
00:03:04.578 --> 00:03:09.323
Digest equality is not privacy repair. Compensation is not reversal.

44
00:03:09.323 --> 00:03:12.170
A receipt is not independent observation.

45
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:17.640
And one exact local inventory is not proof that the inventory was complete.

46
00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.810
The strongest failure modes follow directly: checkpoint hindsight,

47
00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:27.640
regression deletion, evaluator capture, canary contamination, monitor blindness,

48
00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:32.470
partial commit, forward-only migration, authority smuggling, rollback theater,

49
00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.885
irreversibility laundering, rollback cascades,

50
00:03:35.225 --> 00:03:40.405
and repeated promote-rollback trials that hide accumulated cost and residuals.

51
00:03:41.005 --> 00:03:46.652
The chapter has real but narrow evidence. One prospective local campaign restored

52
00:03:46.652 --> 00:03:49.947
fifteen of fifteen declared transaction trees exactly,

53
00:03:49.947 --> 00:03:54.182
while six best-versus-final checkpoint disagreements showed why checkpoint authority

54
00:03:54.182 --> 00:03:56.535
must be fixed in advance.

55
00:03:56.875 --> 00:04:01.523
Another attack-control campaign achieved exact rollback in thirty-two of thirty-six

56
00:04:01.523 --> 00:04:06.171
cases but admitted useful release in only two of thirty-six,

57
00:04:06.171 --> 00:04:08.030
below both release gates.

58
00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:14.605
A larger bounded inventory exercise restored thirty-five of thirty-five named local

59
00:04:14.605 --> 00:04:18.573
surfaces, while immutable corpus, feature, checkpoint, raw-evidence,

60
00:04:18.573 --> 00:04:23.675
simulated remote, and external-descendant surfaces remained unresolved by design.

61
00:04:24.015 --> 00:04:29.716
These are useful tests of record and recovery discipline, not proof of production

62
00:04:29.716 --> 00:04:31.470
usefulness or effect-complete rollback.

63
00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:36.846
The core claim therefore remains Design rationale at argument support.

64
00:04:36.846 --> 00:04:42.100
The maximum justified inference is bounded local non-core governance effect only.

65
00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:48.101
This visual does not establish a useful replacement, production transfer,

66
00:04:48.101 --> 00:04:52.064
semantic recovery, privacy erasure, independent evaluator quality,

67
00:04:52.064 --> 00:04:54.895
safe self-improvement, or chapter-core promotion.

68
00:04:55.235 --> 00:05:01.225
The practical rule is simple: no replacement is complete until identity, evidence,

69
00:05:01.225 --> 00:05:04.220
state, effects, authority, recovery, residuals, descendants,

70
00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.215
and terminal disposition are all explicit.

71
00:05:07.555 --> 00:05:13.062
The next chapter, Security Kernel and Digital SCIFs, asks how privileged execution

72
00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:18.110
can remain isolated while those replacement and recovery obligations are enforced.
